{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789225730,"nonce":"gRtRI5aavYoPMps+tuoQTKB1dsk=","title":"An integer initializer bound to a float annotation keeps its integer type","message":"let x: f64 = 3\n    println(x)          // 3, not 3.0\n    let y: f64 = x * 2\n    println(y)          // 6\n\nThe binding records `f64` for `x` but the value it holds is the i64 the\nliteral lowered to. `narrow_float_to_declared` only reconciles the two\nfloat widths; an integer against a float annotation passes through\nuntouched, so every read of `x` is an integer that the variable table\ncalls a float. Whether an operation on it then computes in float or\ninteger depends on which of the two a given site consults.\n\nSame in the Python frontend: `x: float = 3` prints `3`.\n\nFix: the declared scalar wins for integer-to-float as well, converting\nthe initializer at the binding the way a parameter or return would.","files":null}]}